#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054581 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67e6a951-2645-5440-8059-14f0182933ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/id #uuid "3c7dd3ad-eaa9-5312-adaf-3a0772e845f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-762" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty { {{n}}(x-1)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=1$ of $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty { {{n}}(x-1)^{n}} = (x-1) + {2(x-1)^2} + {3(x-1)^3} +\\cdots .\n$$\n\nTo find $f'(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1+0+4(x-1)+9(x-1)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+4(x-1)+9(x-1)^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054582 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d418552d-d147-5736-8acd-5ba237a90e07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Term for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/id #uuid "cb7a106a-130a-5479-b60d-8ea76a8cd8de" :question/math-academy-id "e-763" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{ (x-3)^{n}}{n}\n$$\n\nFind $a_n,$ given that\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n![](images/af/af9c822ae7190d9d82dbdd5024b5393a912b0cce5af42f39bcb5d37c3e675283.svg)\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n![](images/11/111f8df85bc2af18c21b63b254f6d3b679cb9b3a6a1ce35e9b0e4ef85f96ae07.svg)\nWe can now write the Taylor series for $f'(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (x-3)^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\na_n = (x-3)^{n-1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054583 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88b9751d-450e-5ae9-bda6-1c1d136bc929" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Convergence for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/id #uuid "e656ee8d-55b9-5ea6-beae-7c2caf329a6a" :question/math-academy-id "e-764" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$  about $x=0$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-3)^{n}\\,x^n}{n^3}\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x| <            R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of $f'(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of $f'(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-3)^{n+1}\\,{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{3}}{(-3)^{n}\\,{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-3)x\\cdot {n}^{3}}{(n+1)^{3}}| \\\\  & =3|x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{3}} \\\\  & =3|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3|x| & <1 \\\\ |x| & <\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence is $R = \\dfrac 1 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67e6a951-2645-5440-8059-14f0182933ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f0fdf0e-9fa9-582c-8c4b-9406db77313b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=-(x-2)+\\frac{1}{4}(x-2)^{3}-\\frac{1}{9}(x-2)^{5}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527407} {:answer/id #uuid "4a5b2df0-be56-5973-b10a-9adb13eb7001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=-2(x-2)+\\frac{2}{3}(x-2)^{3}-\\frac{1}{20}(x-2)^{5}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527453} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed9346d-6662-59cd-9127-e14b4f057c1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=-(x-2)+\\frac{1}{3}(x-2)^{3}-\\frac{1}{9}(x-2)^{5}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527459} {:answer/id #uuid "947bb88e-6bd6-5368-be62-f764d069dbdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=-2(x-2)+\\frac{2}{3}(x-2)^{3}-\\frac{4}{9}(x-2)^{5}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527602} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f46b76-d51d-51eb-8c4a-4fa09ccab676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=-2(x-2)+\\frac{1}{5}(x-2)^{3}-\\frac{1}{20}(x-2)^{5}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a5b2df0-be56-5973-b10a-9adb13eb7001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=-2(x-2)+\\frac{2}{3}(x-2)^{3}-\\frac{1}{20}(x-2)^{5}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527453} :answer-field/id #uuid "6804c5fb-1602-564d-9176-9aaeae9972c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "98bdadc3-a767-57cf-925f-bd9117f593a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-126105" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{(2n-1)!}(x-2)^{2n}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=2$ of ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{(2n-1)!}(x-2)^{2n}=-(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}(x-2)^{4}-\\frac{1}{120}(x-2)^{6}+⋯.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-2(x-2)+\\frac{2}{3}(x-2)^{3}-\\frac{1}{20}(x-2)^{5}+⋯.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d418552d-d147-5736-8acd-5ba237a90e07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Term for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "553c551b-959c-5820-87fb-2b069710efc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{2n}}{2n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527468} {:answer/id #uuid "5a5063d3-43ea-5662-b377-57fe02f5d57c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(n+1){x}^{2n}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527480} {:answer/id #uuid "9700df67-9d0d-5e87-9766-a6afb911fb23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(2n+1){x}^{2n+1}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527610} {:answer/id #uuid "ae4aa833-ad3f-5510-ae92-9d1e136d42f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(2n+1){x}^{2n}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527664} {:answer/id #uuid "f07d0cab-5716-55ac-bbae-1ac7c49f272f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(n+1){x}^{2n+1}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae4aa833-ad3f-5510-ae92-9d1e136d42f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(2n+1){x}^{2n}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527664} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9a2bcd2-2dd5-56d9-bb97-fed17319b128" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d825b7e3-8340-5978-bde4-a68ed2d3d297" :question/math-academy-id "q-126164" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{n}.\n$$\n\nFind ${a}_{n},$ given that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{2}+⋯+{{\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{n}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{2}+⋯+{{\\frac{(2n+1){x}^{2n}}{n}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(2n+1){x}^{2n}}{n}\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(2n+1){x}^{2n}}{n}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88b9751d-450e-5ae9-bda6-1c1d136bc929" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Convergence for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18711a67-a492-57d6-a52e-c561827650a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527356} {:answer/id #uuid "7cfc270e-962f-5621-9582-118023c92072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527543} {:answer/id #uuid "98c37dae-ca74-5244-a8e0-66f7bf7db77b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527619} {:answer/id #uuid "f312a360-5080-5146-8b4c-3e911d468ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527782} {:answer/id #uuid "f9fdcc71-0455-521d-9bfd-75d36fbb2b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f312a360-5080-5146-8b4c-3e911d468ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527782} :answer-field/id #uuid "65648b09-5e4d-52de-bda6-67cf60be78b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f964668-eee6-5cc5-b2ad-92e74506589f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126170" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(3x)^{n}}{{2}^{n+1}}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(3x)^{n+1}}{{2}^{n+2}}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{n+1}}{(3x)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{3x}{2}| \\\\  & =\\frac{3|x|}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3|x|}{2} & <1 \\\\ |x| & <\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67e6a951-2645-5440-8059-14f0182933ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e5a7749-718c-5513-a9d5-b57147d75bf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-x+x^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e5a7749-718c-5513-a9d5-b57147d75bf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-x+x^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527406} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bbcf4d6-c0cf-534a-b9b2-ae3a5bb2a2f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f7b083c-c283-53ee-9bad-76a4fd42d736" :question/math-academy-id "q-347640" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n}{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=0$ of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,{x}^{3}+\\,⋯$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n}{x}^{n}=x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+⋯.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=1-x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}+⋯.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527952 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d418552d-d147-5736-8acd-5ba237a90e07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Term for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8e12dbd-e2b8-5ddd-ae75-29b1c5d0d207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3^n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8e12dbd-e2b8-5ddd-ae75-29b1c5d0d207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3^n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527791} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad2f7538-ba88-5617-8a32-3e7d342103d9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "68b82aa8-1837-5666-8db7-ce28e32f59f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-347643" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{{3}^{n}}{n}(x+2)^{n}.\n$$\n\nFind the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-1}}$(-1)^{n+1}:(x+2)^{n-1}$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=3(x+2)-\\frac{9}{2}(x+2)^{2}+⋯+{{(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{{3}^{n}}{n}(x+2)^{n}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=3-9(x+2)+⋯+{{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}(x+2)^{n-1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{3}^{n}(-1)^{n+1}(x+2)^{n-1}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67e6a951-2645-5440-8059-14f0182933ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "468382ee-de8e-5b62-bdba-730e1723390a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+3x+9x^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "468382ee-de8e-5b62-bdba-730e1723390a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+3x+9x^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527444} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dbc4f5f-1cd1-5dfe-85c7-4e553352e3a0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b0cfae1-dde2-5a5f-b5b7-77aa05d10cca" :question/math-academy-id "q-352352" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{n}}{n+1}{x}^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=0$ of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,27{x}^{3}+\\,⋯$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{n}}{n+1}{x}^{n+1}=x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}+\\frac{27}{4}{x}^{4}+⋯.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=1+3x+9{x}^{2}+27{x}^{3}+⋯.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d418552d-d147-5736-8acd-5ba237a90e07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Term for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98d838ad-9b6a-5fe9-993f-42b82cce0af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{3n+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98d838ad-9b6a-5fe9-993f-42b82cce0af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{3n+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527620} :answer-field/id #uuid "e36bb404-de5f-555e-8e97-3e924df8257e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e8579e15-923e-57b1-875d-4092d7ca1f68" :question/math-academy-id "q-352513" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(x-2)^{n}}{3n+4}.\n$$\n\nFind the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-1}}\n$(-1)^{n+1}(x-2)^{n-1}$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x-2}{7}-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+⋯+{{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(x-2)^{n}}{3n+4}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{7}-\\frac{2(x-2)}{10}+⋯+{{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}n(x-2)^{n-1}}{3n+4}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n(-1)^{n+1}(x-2)^{n-1}}{3n+4} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{3n+4}(-1)^{n+1}(x-2)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d418552d-d147-5736-8acd-5ba237a90e07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Term for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69ac432d-af48-5d58-9c4e-6b45fa9907cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{(2n+1)!}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69ac432d-af48-5d58-9c4e-6b45fa9907cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{(2n+1)!}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319716} :answer-field/id #uuid "baaed7d0-ce21-5a59-8834-a8eeeb951918" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba1a2065-05f5-5d7a-9aeb-6e943be02b88" :question/math-academy-id "q-352520" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n}}{(2n+1)!}.\n$$\n\nFind the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-1}}\n$(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{120}+⋯+{{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n}}{(2n+1)!}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{2x}{120}+⋯+{{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}n{x}^{n-1}}{(2n+1)!}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}}{(2n+1)!} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{(2n+1)!}(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d418552d-d147-5736-8acd-5ba237a90e07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Term for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54c93b08-f179-58d2-83b0-1c656ae49846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{n+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54c93b08-f179-58d2-83b0-1c656ae49846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{n+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527467} :answer-field/id #uuid "de0a5eb4-d107-5b19-9ba5-baaaf701916e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4358f8d2-0190-5bf2-b45f-aefdcdef214a" :question/math-academy-id "q-352556" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{6}{n(n+2)}{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nFind the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-1}}$(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}+⋯+{{(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{6}{n(n+2)}{x}^{n}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2-\\frac{3}{2}x+⋯+{{(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{6}{n+2}{x}^{n-1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n+2}(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88b9751d-450e-5ae9-bda6-1c1d136bc929" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Convergence for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03fe077c-2f54-5d1e-9d94-e81bf9e7195c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03fe077c-2f54-5d1e-9d94-e81bf9e7195c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319405} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dfd9f6f-00ee-5561-ae17-a34a75446cf8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bd3fd116-3eb9-5711-87df-f837fd78e515" :question/math-academy-id "q-352603" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(4n-1){(\\frac{x}{4})}^{n}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$\n\nThe radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(4(n+1)-1){(\\frac{x}{4})}^{n+1}}{(4n-1){(\\frac{x}{4})}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{4n+3}{4n-1}||\\frac{x}{4}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|x|}{4} & <1 \\\\ |x| & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is $R=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88b9751d-450e-5ae9-bda6-1c1d136bc929" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Convergence for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51e8fb2f-fb5d-5f28-bb8f-bf604bccc7dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51e8fb2f-fb5d-5f28-bb8f-bf604bccc7dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527464} :answer-field/id #uuid "0560cfb9-c1b9-5465-8ef1-a2f3a5215585" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f4bbe9de-e1d4-5432-b7cd-e16cf2a892fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-352604" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n}\\sqrt{n}{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{n}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$\n\nThe radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}\\sqrt{n+1}{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{n+1}}{(-1)^{n}\\sqrt{n}{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\sqrt{n+1}}{\\sqrt{n}}||\\frac{x}{6}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|x|}{6} & <1 \\\\ |x| & <6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is $R=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88b9751d-450e-5ae9-bda6-1c1d136bc929" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Convergence for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b686bb7a-72f2-59c9-a77f-e70b5f80dc08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b686bb7a-72f2-59c9-a77f-e70b5f80dc08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527681} :answer-field/id #uuid "83c1d007-5db9-5493-8b1e-8b3d9c5489b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3dadad8-be2e-5983-9aeb-5a80535c1cf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-352615" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\sqrt{5n+2}\\,(-4x)^{n}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$\n\nThe radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\sqrt{5(n+1)+2}\\,(-4x)^{n+1}}{\\sqrt{5n+2}\\,(-4x)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\sqrt{5n+7}}{\\sqrt{5n+2}}||(-4x)| \\\\  & =4|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4|x| & <1 \\\\ |x| & <\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is $R=\\frac{1}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527948 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67e6a951-2645-5440-8059-14f0182933ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "045d85d5-70f1-56bd-94b2-61961f261fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{6}x-\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{3}-⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527317} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed03b02-68eb-53cf-9454-a0c9af1aca7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}x+\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}-⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527550} {:answer/id #uuid "95e21e4a-6100-5b55-bbea-c273e13f2df6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{6}x-\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527605} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f1c5aa-ebaf-5249-aacd-029001be6206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=1-3x+12{x}^{2}-⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527645} {:answer/id #uuid "a47e49ad-db53-5db7-8bf0-c96364cabc51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{2}{9}x+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a47e49ad-db53-5db7-8bf0-c96364cabc51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{2}{9}x+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527646} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e78dbe4-3489-5a9a-a897-22956fb2e946" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c748f35-86fe-5e16-bc4c-e8e0b39f4472" :question/math-academy-id "q-50863" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{3n}{x}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=0$ of ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{3n}{x}^{n-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{6}x-\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{3}-⋯.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=0+\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{2}{9}x+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-⋯.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67e6a951-2645-5440-8059-14f0182933ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b4a816e-e965-5c13-862c-ef92f7d9612c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527330} {:answer/id #uuid "58be9b03-2a3f-5cc8-aaff-3870fa2f15cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527477} {:answer/id #uuid "8de11366-71a0-5170-afd9-77957cb9579a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=1+\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527588} {:answer/id #uuid "99662a1b-8e22-50b8-b393-8f7bcf03d54d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{12}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527621} {:answer/id #uuid "9af04276-8d03-5d33-8e2c-d101fe1a5bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{x}{8}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b4a816e-e965-5c13-862c-ef92f7d9612c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527330} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f3afd52-c066-5ff8-8ba0-53490bbad92b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d411bca7-2808-5bc3-9792-16db8d0254d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-50865" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{4n}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=0$ of ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{4n}=\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{12}+⋯.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+⋯.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88b9751d-450e-5ae9-bda6-1c1d136bc929" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Convergence for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0f7271c-5268-55b5-8703-f3cc507f5874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053386} {:answer/id #uuid "ed487fda-82b6-53a6-958c-56f559cf6e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053387} {:answer/id #uuid "35f7caa3-964e-5150-ab9e-6fc9acbcff36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053388} {:answer/id #uuid "6bd68ceb-0543-55ca-92e6-971ba7ed329b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053390} {:answer/id #uuid "6a61c0f3-d375-5c93-bfaa-1af4668c9cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a61c0f3-d375-5c93-bfaa-1af4668c9cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527510} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ec3575f-fb34-5109-b19d-e6be26a7a0df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ca8106e-3840-584b-9cd7-ab688b28310f" :question/math-academy-id "q-50910" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n-1}}{n!}{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the Taylor series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$ \n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\\cdot \\frac{n!}{(-1)^{n-1}{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)x\\cdot n!}{(n+1)!}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{|x|}{n+1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$  Since $0<1$ for all $x,$ we conclude that \n$R=\\infty .$"}]}
